-- 1. [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · Skill 2 · ans A] id 27921f88-06dd-4dc2-b379-44128298a624
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A \(0.5000\ \text{g}\) sample of a copper-bearing alloy is dissolved, and all of its copper is converted to \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\). Excess iodide is then added, liberating iodine by \(2\,\text{Cu}^{2+} + 4\,\text{I}^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{CuI}(s) + \text{I}_2\). The liberated \(\text{I}_2\) is titrated to the starch endpoint with \(0.1500\ \text{M}\) thiosulfate, \(\text{I}_2 + 2\,\text{S}_2\text{O}_3^{2-} \rightarrow 2\,\text{I}^- + \text{S}_4\text{O}_6^{2-}\), requiring \(20.00\ \text{mL}\). Taking the atomic mass of copper as \(63.55\ \text{g/mol}\), what is the mass percent of copper in the alloy, most nearly?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(38.13\\%\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(19.06\\%\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(76.26\\%\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(9.53\\%\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the two redox half-reactions must be chained to establish the controlling mole ratio between copper and thiosulfate before any mass can be computed. In the first reaction each \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) (\(+2\)) is reduced to \(\text{Cu}^{+}\) in \(\text{CuI}\) (\(+1\)), gaining one electron, while two iodide ions are oxidized to \(\text{I}_2\); the electron balance fixes the ratio at \(2\,\text{Cu}^{2+} : 1\,\text{I}_2\). In the titration, \(\text{I}_2\) (oxidation state \(0\)) gains a total of two electrons to form \(2\,\text{I}^-\), matching the two electrons released as the sulfur of two thiosulfate ions (average \(+2\)) is oxidized to tetrathionate (average \(+2.5\)), fixing the ratio at \(1\,\text{I}_2 : 2\,\text{S}_2\text{O}_3^{2-}\). Combining the two ratios, the factor of two cancels and \(n(\text{Cu}^{2+}) = n(\text{S}_2\text{O}_3^{2-})\). The titration delivers \(0.1500\ \text{M} \times 0.02000\ \text{L} = 3.000\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\) thiosulfate, so \(n(\text{Cu}) = 3.000\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\). The mass of copper is \(3.000\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol} \times 63.55\ \text{g/mol} = 0.1906\ \text{g}\), giving \(0.1906\,/\,0.5000 \times 100 = 38.13\%\). (Choice B) \(19.06\%\) results from using the ratio \(\text{Cu} : \text{I}_2 = 1{:}1\), thereby dropping the \(2\,\text{Cu}^{2+}\) per \(\text{I}_2\) required by the first half-reaction; this halves the copper count to the moles of \(\text{I}_2\) (\(1.500\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\)). (Choice C) \(76.26\%\) arises from taking the titration mole ratio as \(\text{I}_2 : \text{S}_2\text{O}_3^{2-} = 1{:}1\) instead of \(1{:}2\); this doubles the iodine to \(3.000\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\) and then, with the correct \(2\,\text{Cu}\) per \(\text{I}_2\), doubles the copper to \(6.000\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\). (Choice D) \(9.53\%\) comes from inverting the first reaction so that one \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) liberates two \(\text{I}_2\) (a \(\text{Cu}:\text{I}_2\) ratio of \(1:2\)), which gives \(n(\text{Cu}) = n(\text{I}_2)/2 = 7.5\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}\) — one-quarter of the thiosulfate moles — for a copper mass of \(0.0477\ \text{g}\), or \(9.53\%\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must extract the two electron-transfer ratios from the half-reactions, chain them to obtain the governing copper-to-thiosulfate stoichiometry, and carry the intermediate mole quantity through to a final mass percent.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '27921f88-06dd-4dc2-b379-44128298a624';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '27921f88-06dd-4dc2-b379-44128298a624';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('27921f88-06dd-4dc2-b379-44128298a624', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Drops the 2:1 copper-to-iodine ratio from the first half-reaction, taking Cu:I2 as 1:1 so the copper count is halved to the moles of I2.$a$),
  ('27921f88-06dd-4dc2-b379-44128298a624', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Takes the titration mole ratio I2:thiosulfate as 1:1 rather than 1:2, which doubles the iodine and then the copper through the correct 2 Cu per I2 step.$a$),
  ('27921f88-06dd-4dc2-b379-44128298a624', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the first reaction stoichiometry so one Cu2+ yields two I2 (Cu:I2 = 1:2), halving the copper relative to the iodine liberated (n(Cu)=n(I2)/2).$a$);

-- 2. [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · Skill 2 · ans C] id 35a7c131-0cb4-4980-a402-145cefa64b59
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$White phosphorus disproportionates in hot concentrated base, where elemental \(\text{P}_4\) is converted to phosphine gas, \(\text{PH}_3\), and hypophosphite ion, \(\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\), according to the unbalanced skeleton \(\text{P}_4 \rightarrow \text{PH}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\) (basic). A student balances this process by the half-reaction method, balancing oxygen with \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and hydrogen with \(\text{OH}^-\) as appropriate for the basic medium, equalizes the electrons, and combines the half-reactions into one overall equation written with the smallest whole-number coefficients. If \(0.20\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{P}_4\) reacts completely, how many moles of \(\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\) are most likely produced?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.20\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.40\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.60\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.80\\ \\text{mol}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the fully balanced equation fixes a \(3{:}1\) mole ratio of \(\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\) to \(\text{P}_4\), so \(0.20\ \text{mol}\ \text{P}_4 \times 3 = 0.60\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\). Phosphorus enters at oxidation state \(0\) in \(\text{P}_4\). In \(\text{PH}_3\) (H is \(+1\)) phosphorus is \(-3\), so the reduction half-reaction in base is \(\text{P}_4 + 12\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 12e^- \rightarrow 4\,\text{PH}_3 + 12\,\text{OH}^-\), a gain of \(3e^-\) per phosphorus. In \(\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\) (two H at \(+1\), two O at \(-2\), overall \(-1\)) phosphorus is \(+1\), so the oxidation half-reaction in base is \(\text{P}_4 + 8\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow 4\,\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^- + 4e^-\), a loss of \(1e^-\) per phosphorus. The controlling feature is the asymmetric electron split: each reduced P consumes \(3e^-\) while each oxidized P releases only \(1e^-\), so the electrons balance only when one P is reduced for every three P oxidized. Equalizing electrons (\(\text{LCM}\) of \(12\) and \(4\) is \(12\)) means multiplying the oxidation half by \(3\); adding the two and cancelling the \(12e^-\) and the spectator water gives \(4\,\text{P}_4 + 12\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 12\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow 4\,\text{PH}_3 + 12\,\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\). Dividing every coefficient by \(4\) to reach the smallest whole numbers yields \(\text{P}_4 + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{PH}_3 + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\). This balances: P \(4 = 1 + 3\); O \(6 = 6\); H \(9 = 3 + 6\); and charge \(-3 = -3\) on each side. With \(\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-{:}\text{P}_4 = 3{:}1\), \(0.20\ \text{mol}\ \text{P}_4\) gives \(0.60\ \text{mol}\ \text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\). (Choice A) \(0.20\ \text{mol}\) follows from a reversed \(3{:}1\) split sending three P to \(\text{PH}_3\) and one P to \(\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\) (a \(1{:}1\) ratio of hypophosphite to \(\text{P}_4\)); this releases \(1e^-\) against \(9e^-\) demanded and likewise violates electron conservation. (Choice B) \(0.40\ \text{mol}\) follows from assuming an even \(2{:}2\) split, \(\text{P}_4 \rightarrow 2\,\text{PH}_3 + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\); that split releases only \(2e^-\) (two P oxidized at \(1e^-\)) against \(6e^-\) demanded (two P reduced at \(3e^-\)), so it fails to conserve electrons. (Choice D) \(0.80\ \text{mol}\) follows from sending all four phosphorus atoms to \(\text{H}_2\text{PO}_2^-\) (a \(4{:}1\) ratio); with no reduction product there is no electron acceptor, so no balanced disproportionation exists. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must assign oxidation states, construct and electron-balance two half-reactions in base, reduce the combined equation to smallest whole numbers, and then apply the resulting mole ratio to a given quantity.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '35a7c131-0cb4-4980-a402-145cefa64b59';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '35a7c131-0cb4-4980-a402-145cefa64b59';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('35a7c131-0cb4-4980-a402-145cefa64b59', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the asymmetric electron split, sending three P to the reduction product and one to the oxidation product, giving a 1:1 hypophosphite-to-P4 ratio$a$),
  ('35a7c131-0cb4-4980-a402-145cefa64b59', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Assumes a symmetric 2:2 disproportionation split (P4 -> 2 PH3 + 2 H2PO2-) without electron-balancing, yielding a 2:1 hypophosphite-to-P4 ratio$a$),
  ('35a7c131-0cb4-4980-a402-145cefa64b59', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Sends all four phosphorus atoms to the oxidation product (4:1 ratio), ignoring that disproportionation requires a simultaneous reduction product$a$);

-- 3. [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · Skill 2 · ans D] id 95233727-fdae-4c9d-a69b-34d8094f1a27
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$In a strongly basic solution, permanganate oxidizes sulfite to sulfate while it is itself reduced to manganese(IV) oxide, summarized by the skeleton change \(\text{MnO}_4^- + \text{SO}_3^{2-} \rightarrow \text{MnO}_2 + \text{SO}_4^{2-}\) (basic). A student balances each half-reaction by the half-reaction method (balancing O with \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and H with \(\text{OH}^-\) as appropriate for base), equalizes the electrons, then combines them into a single overall equation written with the smallest whole-number coefficients. In that overall equation, what is the coefficient of \(\text{OH}^-\)?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because two half-reactions can be added only after their electrons are equalized, and any \(\text{OH}^-\) that appears on both sides of the summed equation must then be cancelled before the smallest whole-number coefficients are read. First identify the correct products in base: manganese goes from \(+7\) in \(\text{MnO}_4^-\) to \(+4\) in \(\text{MnO}_2\), a gain of only \(3\) electrons, giving the reduction half-reaction \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{MnO}_2 + 4\text{OH}^-\). Sulfur goes from \(+4\) in \(\text{SO}_3^{2-}\) to \(+6\) in \(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\), a loss of \(2\) electrons, giving the oxidation half-reaction \(\text{SO}_3^{2-} + 2\text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{SO}_4^{2-} + \text{H}_2\text{O} + 2e^-\). The least common multiple of \(3\) and \(2\) is \(6\), so the reduction half-reaction is multiplied by \(2\) and the oxidation half-reaction by \(3\): \(2\text{MnO}_4^- + 4\text{H}_2\text{O} + 6e^- \rightarrow 2\text{MnO}_2 + 8\text{OH}^-\) and \(3\text{SO}_3^{2-} + 6\text{OH}^- \rightarrow 3\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 3\text{H}_2\text{O} + 6e^-\). Adding these cancels the \(6\) electrons and leaves \(\text{OH}^-\) on both sides: \(8\,\text{OH}^-\) produced on the right and \(6\,\text{OH}^-\) consumed on the left, for a net \(2\,\text{OH}^-\) on the right; likewise \(4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) on the left minus \(3\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) on the right leaves \(1\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) on the left. The overall equation is therefore \(2\text{MnO}_4^- + 3\text{SO}_3^{2-} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\text{MnO}_2 + 3\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 2\text{OH}^-\), which balances for all atoms (O: \(18 = 18\); H: \(2 = 2\)) and for charge (\(-8\) on each side) with no common factor, so the coefficient of \(\text{OH}^-\) is \(2\). (Choice A) \(4\) is the number of \(\text{OH}^-\) in the single, unscaled reduction half-reaction \(\text{MnO}_4^- + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{MnO}_2 + 4\text{OH}^-\); reading that value directly ignores both the required doubling of the half-reaction and the subsequent cancellation of \(\text{OH}^-\) against the oxidation side. (Choice B) \(8\) is the amount of \(\text{OH}^-\) produced by the doubled reduction half-reaction alone; it results from correctly scaling but then failing to subtract the \(6\,\text{OH}^-\) consumed by the tripled oxidation half-reaction. (Choice C) \(6\) arises from incorrectly using the acidic five-electron reduction of permanganate to \(\text{Mn}^{2+}\) instead of the three-electron basic reduction to \(\text{MnO}_2\); that wrong product forces a least common multiple of \(10\) and yields a net \(6\,\text{OH}^-\), so the value reflects the wrong manganese product rather than the species actually formed in base. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the correct basic-medium product and electron count for each half-reaction with the scaling-and-cancellation steps to obtain the reduced overall stoichiometry.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '95233727-fdae-4c9d-a69b-34d8094f1a27';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '95233727-fdae-4c9d-a69b-34d8094f1a27';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('95233727-fdae-4c9d-a69b-34d8094f1a27', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Reads the OH- coefficient straight off the single unscaled reduction half-reaction, skipping both the doubling and the cross-side cancellation required when the half-reactions are combined.$a$),
  ('95233727-fdae-4c9d-a69b-34d8094f1a27', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly scales the reduction half-reaction to give 8 OH- but omits the final cancellation of the 6 OH- consumed by the oxidation half-reaction, stopping one step short of the smallest whole-number equation.$a$),
  ('95233727-fdae-4c9d-a69b-34d8094f1a27', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Uses the famous acidic five-electron reduction of permanganate to Mn2+ instead of the three-electron basic reduction to MnO2, producing the wrong least common multiple and a net OH- coefficient of 6.$a$);

-- 4. [Balancing Redox (Half-Reaction Method) · Skill 2 · ans B] id e87440a4-dd10-4650-8154-cd118570f2d4
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$In acidic solution, dichromate ion quantitatively oxidizes sulfite to sulfate while being reduced to \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\), as in the skeleton process \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + \text{SO}_3^{2-} + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Cr}^{3+} + \text{SO}_4^{2-} + \text{H}_2\text{O}\). A student balances each half-reaction separately by the half-reaction method, equalizes the electrons transferred, and then combines them, canceling any species that appears on both sides, into one overall equation with the smallest whole-number coefficients. In that overall equation, what is the coefficient of \(\text{H}^+\)?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"14"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the two half-reactions must be balanced independently, scaled to equal electrons, and then combined with cancellation of species common to both sides. The reduction half-reaction is \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 14\text{H}^+ + 6e^- \rightarrow 2\text{Cr}^{3+} + 7\text{H}_2\text{O}\): each of the two chromium atoms goes from \(+6\) to \(+3\), for \(2 \times 3 = 6\) electrons gained, and 14 \(\text{H}^+\) are needed to convert the seven oxygens to water. The oxidation half-reaction is \(\text{SO}_3^{2-} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{SO}_4^{2-} + 2\text{H}^+ + 2e^-\): sulfur goes from \(+4\) to \(+6\), losing 2 electrons, and one water supplies the extra oxygen, releasing 2 \(\text{H}^+\). Equalizing electrons requires \(\text{LCM}(6,2)=6\), so the sulfite half-reaction is multiplied by 3: \(3\text{SO}_3^{2-} + 3\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 3\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 6\text{H}^+ + 6e^-\). Adding the two half-reactions places 14 \(\text{H}^+\) on the left and 6 \(\text{H}^+\) on the right; these partially cancel to leave a net \(14 - 6 = 8\,\text{H}^+\) on the left. (The waters likewise reduce from 7 on the right and 3 on the left to a net 4 on the right.) The balanced overall equation is \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-} + 3\text{SO}_3^{2-} + 8\text{H}^+ \rightarrow 2\text{Cr}^{3+} + 3\text{SO}_4^{2-} + 4\text{H}_2\text{O}\), so the coefficient of \(\text{H}^+\) is \(8\). (Choice A) 6 is the number of \(\text{H}^+\) generated on the product side by the tripled sulfite oxidation half-reaction alone; it ignores the 14 \(\text{H}^+\) consumed by the dichromate reduction with which it must be combined. (Choice C) 12 results from failing to scale the sulfite half-reaction to match electrons: pairing one (unscaled) sulfite, which releases only 2 \(\text{H}^+\), against the reduction's 14 \(\text{H}^+\) gives \(14 - 2 = 12\), but this leaves the electrons unbalanced (6 versus 2) and is not a valid combined equation. (Choice D) 14 is the number of \(\text{H}^+\) in the dichromate reduction half-reaction by itself; it treats the reduction's protons as final and overlooks that the oxidation half-reaction also produces \(\text{H}^+\) on the opposite side, which must be subtracted. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must balance two distinct half-reactions, determine the electron-equalizing multipliers, and then track how protons on opposite sides cancel to obtain the net coefficient.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'e87440a4-dd10-4650-8154-cd118570f2d4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e87440a4-dd10-4650-8154-cd118570f2d4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e87440a4-dd10-4650-8154-cd118570f2d4', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Reports the H+ produced by the scaled oxidation half-reaction alone, omitting cancellation against the reduction half-reaction's 14 H+.$a$),
  ('e87440a4-dd10-4650-8154-cd118570f2d4', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Skips equalizing electrons and pairs one unscaled sulfite (2 H+) against the reduction's 14 H+, computing 14 - 2 = 12.$a$),
  ('e87440a4-dd10-4650-8154-cd118570f2d4', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Stops at the dichromate reduction half-reaction's 14 H+ and fails to subtract the H+ generated on the opposite side by the oxidation half-reaction.$a$);

-- 5. [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents · Skill 2 · ans C] id 1ef6143a-8013-4392-a87a-3a015a93a4e8
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist dissolves a \(1.27\,\text{g}\) sample of pure copper metal (molar mass \(63.5\,\text{g/mol}\)) in an excess of concentrated nitric acid, \(\text{HNO}_3\), and observes the evolution of a brown gas as the only gaseous product. Assuming the reaction goes to completion and forms \(\text{Cu(NO}_3)_2\), how many moles of \(\text{HNO}_3\) are most likely consumed in total?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.0400\\,\\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.0533\\,\\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.0800\\,\\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.0600\\,\\text{mol}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the calculation must chain three steps: choosing the correct reduction product, balancing the redox equation, and accounting for the dual role of \(\text{HNO}_3\). First, the brown gas fixes the reduction product: concentrated \(\text{HNO}_3\) reduces to \(\text{NO}_2\) (the brown gas), in which oxygen is \(-2\) each (\(-4\) total) on a neutral molecule, so nitrogen is \(+4\); nitrogen therefore drops from \(+5\) in \(\text{HNO}_3\) to \(+4\), gaining one electron per N. Copper is oxidized \(\text{Cu} \rightarrow \text{Cu}^{2+} + 2\text{e}^-\), so two \(\text{NO}_2\) form per copper to balance the two electrons: \(\text{Cu} + 4\,\text{HNO}_3 \rightarrow \text{Cu(NO}_3)_2 + 2\,\text{NO}_2 + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). The crucial discriminator is that the coefficient on \(\text{HNO}_3\) is \(4\), not \(2\): two of the four \(\text{HNO}_3\) are reduced to \(\text{NO}_2\) (the oxidizing role), while the other two are not reduced at all but supply the two \(\text{NO}_3^-\) counterions in \(\text{Cu(NO}_3)_2\) (the acid/spectator role). Finally, moles of copper \(= 1.27\,\text{g} \div 63.5\,\text{g/mol} = 0.0200\,\text{mol}\), so total \(\text{HNO}_3 = 4 \times 0.0200 = 0.0800\,\text{mol}\). (Choice A) \(0.0400\,\text{mol}\) counts only the two \(\text{HNO}_3\) per copper that are reduced to \(\text{NO}_2\) and omits the two nitrate ions that leave as the counterions of \(\text{Cu(NO}_3)_2\); this drops half of the acid actually consumed. (Choice B) \(0.0533\,\text{mol}\) results from using the dilute-acid stoichiometry \(3\,\text{Cu} + 8\,\text{HNO}_3 \rightarrow 3\,\text{Cu(NO}_3)_2 + 2\,\text{NO} + 4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\) (giving \(\tfrac{8}{3} \times 0.0200\,\text{mol}\)), but that equation produces colorless \(\text{NO}\) (N at \(+2\)) rather than the observed brown \(\text{NO}_2\), so it applies the wrong reduction product for concentrated acid. (Choice D) \(0.0600\,\text{mol}\) (\(3 \times 0.0200\,\text{mol}\)) arises from correctly counting the two reduced \(\text{HNO}_3\) but allotting only one nitrate counterion to the copper, treating the product as a \(+1\) species rather than \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\), which requires two nitrate counterions. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify the controlling constraint set by the brown gas, balance the redox process to find the electron and counterion bookkeeping, and carry an intermediate mole result through to a final stoichiometric total.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '1ef6143a-8013-4392-a87a-3a015a93a4e8';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1ef6143a-8013-4392-a87a-3a015a93a4e8';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1ef6143a-8013-4392-a87a-3a015a93a4e8', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Counts only the HNO3 reduced to NO2 (the oxidizing role) and drops the two nitrate ions acting as Cu(NO3)2 counterions$a$),
  ('1ef6143a-8013-4392-a87a-3a015a93a4e8', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Applies dilute-HNO3 stoichiometry (product NO, 8/3 HNO3 per Cu) despite the brown gas signaling concentrated-acid NO2$a$),
  ('1ef6143a-8013-4392-a87a-3a015a93a4e8', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Assigns only one nitrate counterion to copper (treats it as Cu+ not Cu2+), giving 3 HNO3 per Cu instead of 4$a$);

-- 6. [Common Oxidizing & Reducing Agents · Skill 2 · ans D] id 394a6072-6cfc-4f81-999d-f0e11b4daa9c
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist must reduce every aldehyde and ketone group in \(0.020\ \text{mol}\) of 3,5-dioxopentanoic acid, \(\text{HOOC}\text{CH}_2\text{C(=O)CH}_2\text{CHO}\), to the corresponding alcohols using sodium borohydride, \(\text{NaBH}_4\), in methanol, while leaving any group that \(\text{NaBH}_4\) cannot reduce untouched. Assuming each \(\text{NaBH}_4\) can deliver all four of its \(\text{B}-\text{H}\) hydrides and that reducing one carbonyl to an alcohol consumes exactly one hydride, what is the minimum amount of \(\text{NaBH}_4\) most likely required?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.005\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.015\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.040\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.010\\ \\text{mol}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the problem must be solved in three chained steps. First, identify which groups \(\text{NaBH}_4\) actually reduces: \(\text{NaBH}_4\) is a mild hydride donor that reduces the electrophilic carbonyls of aldehydes and ketones but does not reduce the much less electrophilic, resonance-stabilized (and in methanol, deprotonated) carboxylic acid carbonyl. In 3,5-dioxopentanoic acid the C5 aldehyde and the C3 ketone are reduced, while the C1 carboxylic acid survives, so there are \(2\) reducible carbonyls per molecule, not \(3\). Second, compute the hydride demand: \(0.020\ \text{mol} \times 2\ \text{hydride/molecule} = 0.040\ \text{mol}\) of hydride, since each \(\text{C=O} \rightarrow \text{C}-\text{OH}\) reduction consumes one \(\text{H}^-\). Third, convert hydride demand into reagent: because each \(\text{NaBH}_4\) carries four \(\text{B}-\text{H}\) bonds and can deliver all four hydrides, the minimum reagent needed is \(0.040\ \text{mol hydride} \div 4\ \text{hydride/NaBH}_4 = 0.010\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{NaBH}_4\). (Choice A) \(0.005\ \text{mol}\) inverts the reactivity order, assuming the carboxylic acid is the lone group reduced while the aldehyde and ketone are spared \((0.020 \times 1 \div 4)\); in reality the aldehyde and ketone are the groups \(\text{NaBH}_4\) reduces and the acid is the group it leaves intact. (Choice B) \(0.015\ \text{mol}\) results from dropping the chemoselectivity constraint and counting all three carbonyls (including the carboxylic acid) as reducible, giving \(0.020 \times 3 \div 4\); but \(\text{NaBH}_4\) cannot reduce the carboxylic acid, so only two carbonyls per molecule consume hydride. (Choice C) \(0.040\ \text{mol}\) is the hydride demand itself, obtained by treating each \(\text{NaBH}_4\) as supplying only a single hydride \((0.020 \times 2 \div 1)\); this ignores that one borohydride unit can transfer all four of its \(\text{B}-\text{H}\) hydrides. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the chemoselectivity of a specific reducing agent with a multi-step hydride-stoichiometry calculation in which the count of reducible groups feeds the hydride demand, which in turn feeds the reagent amount.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '394a6072-6cfc-4f81-999d-f0e11b4daa9c';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '394a6072-6cfc-4f81-999d-f0e11b4daa9c';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('394a6072-6cfc-4f81-999d-f0e11b4daa9c', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the reactivity order, assuming only the carboxylic acid is reduced while the aldehyde and ketone survive, giving 0.020*1/4 = 0.005 mol$a$),
  ('394a6072-6cfc-4f81-999d-f0e11b4daa9c', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Drops the chemoselectivity constraint and counts the carboxylic acid as reducible (3 carbonyls), giving 0.020*3/4 = 0.015 mol$a$),
  ('394a6072-6cfc-4f81-999d-f0e11b4daa9c', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Treats each NaBH4 as delivering only one hydride instead of four, returning the raw hydride demand 0.020*2 = 0.040 mol as the reagent amount$a$);

-- 7. [Disproportionation Reactions · Skill 2 · ans A] id 33d115a4-8b25-4346-bcc7-9484407f9653
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$When potassium chlorate is heated under controlled conditions, the chlorine atoms redistribute between two products without any oxygen gas escaping, following the unbalanced skeleton \(\text{KClO}_3 \rightarrow \text{KClO}_4 + \text{KCl}\). A technician decomposes a \(122.55\ \text{g}\) sample of pure \(\text{KClO}_3\) (molar mass \(122.55\ \text{g/mol}\)) to completion by this pathway. Using the electron bookkeeping that governs how the chlorine partitions, what mass of \(\text{KClO}_4\) (molar mass \(138.55\ \text{g/mol}\)) is most likely produced?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(103.91\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(91.91\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(34.64\\ \\text{g}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(138.55\\ \\text{g}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the disproportionation electron balance fixes the product ratio, which then drives the mass calculation. Assign oxidation states with K at \(+1\) and O at \(-2\): in \(\text{KClO}_3\), \((+1)+\text{Cl}+3(-2)=0\) gives \(\text{Cl}=+5\) (the intermediate state); in \(\text{KClO}_4\), \((+1)+\text{Cl}+4(-2)=0\) gives \(\text{Cl}=+7\) (oxidized, losing \(2\) electrons per Cl); in \(\text{KCl}\), \(\text{Cl}=-1\) (reduced, gaining \(6\) electrons per Cl). For electrons lost to equal electrons gained, the oxidized-to-reduced atom ratio must satisfy \(2\times(\text{oxidized})=6\times(\text{reduced})\), so oxidized:reduced \(=3:1\). The balanced equation is therefore \(4\,\text{KClO}_3 \rightarrow 3\,\text{KClO}_4 + \text{KCl}\): three of every four chlorine atoms become \(\text{KClO}_4\). The \(122.55\ \text{g}\) sample is \(122.55/122.55 = 1.000\ \text{mol}\) \(\text{KClO}_3\), which yields \(1.000 \times \tfrac{3}{4} = 0.750\ \text{mol}\) \(\text{KClO}_4\); multiplying by \(138.55\ \text{g/mol}\) gives \(0.750 \times 138.55 = 103.91\ \text{g}\). (Choice B) \(91.91\ \text{g}\) applies the correct \(0.750\ \text{mol}\) of product but multiplies by the reactant molar mass \(122.55\ \text{g/mol}\) instead of the \(\text{KClO}_4\) molar mass, conflating the two formulas. (Choice C) \(34.64\ \text{g}\) reverses the product ratio, treating \(\text{KClO}_4\) as the minor product and using \(\tfrac{1}{4}\) mol \((0.250 \times 138.55)\); this would require the \(+7\) oxidation to consume more electrons than the \(-1\) reduction, the opposite of the \(2\)-vs-\(6\) electron balance, which forces \(\text{KClO}_4\) to be the major product. (Choice D) \(138.55\ \text{g}\) ignores the disproportionation stoichiometry entirely, assuming a \(1{:}1\) mole conversion of \(\text{KClO}_3\) to \(\text{KClO}_4\); because one of every four chlorine atoms is instead reduced to \(\text{KCl}\), only three-quarters of the chlorine reaches the \(+7\) product. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain oxidation-state assignment, an electron-balance constraint that fixes the coefficients, and a mole-to-mass conversion, where the intermediate \(3{:}1\) ratio feeds the final calculation.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '33d115a4-8b25-4346-bcc7-9484407f9653';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '33d115a4-8b25-4346-bcc7-9484407f9653';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('33d115a4-8b25-4346-bcc7-9484407f9653', 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Used the correct 0.750 mol of product but multiplied by the reactant (KClO3) molar mass instead of the KClO4 molar mass$a$),
  ('33d115a4-8b25-4346-bcc7-9484407f9653', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reversed the disproportionation electron balance, treating the oxidation product KClO4 as the minor (1/4) product instead of the major (3/4) product$a$),
  ('33d115a4-8b25-4346-bcc7-9484407f9653', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Dropped the disproportionation stoichiometry and assumed a 1:1 mole conversion of KClO3 to KClO4$a$);

-- 8. [Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions · Skill 2 · ans A] id 07fcb5de-d031-47bd-84f9-346fc47457cd
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist neutralizes aqueous barium hydroxide with aqueous acetic acid according to the balanced molecular equation \(\text{Ba(OH)}_2(aq) + 2\,\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Ba(CH}_3\text{COO)}_2(aq) + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}(l)\). Both barium salts are freely soluble strong electrolytes. Which choice most likely gives the correct net ionic equation for this reaction, written with the smallest whole-number coefficients?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}(aq) + \\text{OH}^-(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{CH}_3\\text{COO}^-(aq) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(2\\,\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}(aq) + 2\\,\\text{OH}^-(aq) \\rightarrow 2\\,\\text{CH}_3\\text{COO}^-(aq) + 2\\,\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{H}^+(aq) + \\text{OH}^-(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Ba(OH)}_2(aq) + 2\\,\\text{CH}_3\\text{COOH}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Ba}^{2+}(aq) + 2\\,\\text{CH}_3\\text{COO}^-(aq) + 2\\,\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because constructing the net ionic equation requires chaining several decisions correctly. First, only strong electrolytes are split into ions: \(\text{Ba(OH)}_2\) is a strong base and dissociates fully to \(\text{Ba}^{2+} + 2\,\text{OH}^-\), and the soluble salt \(\text{Ba(CH}_3\text{COO)}_2\) dissociates fully to \(\text{Ba}^{2+} + 2\,\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\); acetic acid, \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\), is a weak acid (a weak electrolyte) and is kept as an intact molecule, while liquid water is molecular. The complete ionic equation is therefore \(\text{Ba}^{2+}(aq) + 2\,\text{OH}^-(aq) + 2\,\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Ba}^{2+}(aq) + 2\,\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-(aq) + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}(l)\). The \(\text{Ba}^{2+}\) ion appears unchanged on both sides and is cancelled as the spectator, leaving \(2\,\text{CH}_3\text{COOH} + 2\,\text{OH}^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^- + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). The final required step is reducing to smallest whole-number coefficients: every coefficient shares a common factor of \(2\), so dividing through gives \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}(aq) + \text{OH}^-(aq) \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-(aq) + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l)\). (Choice B) This is the correct equation after cancelling the \(\text{Ba}^{2+}\) spectator but before dividing out the common factor of \(2\); a net ionic equation must be expressed in the smallest whole-number coefficients, so coefficients of \(2\) on every species are not in lowest terms. (Choice C) This treats \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) as a strong acid and writes it as a free \(\text{H}^+\) ion; because acetic acid is a weak electrolyte that exists predominantly as undissociated molecules, it must remain intact, and reducing it to \(\text{H}^+ + \text{OH}^- \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{O}\) incorrectly erases the weak-acid molecule from the equation. (Choice D) This keeps the strong base \(\text{Ba(OH)}_2\) as an intact molecular unit on the left rather than dissociating it into \(\text{Ba}^{2+}\) and \(\text{OH}^-\); a strong base is a strong electrolyte and must be split into ions, after which the \(\text{Ba}^{2+}\) cancels as a spectator and the hydroxide acting on the weak acid is revealed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate electrolyte classification, spectator-ion cancellation, and coefficient reduction in sequence to arrive at the correct net ionic equation.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '07fcb5de-d031-47bd-84f9-346fc47457cd';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '07fcb5de-d031-47bd-84f9-346fc47457cd';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('07fcb5de-d031-47bd-84f9-346fc47457cd', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Cancels the spectator ion correctly but forgets the final step of dividing all coefficients by their common factor, leaving the equation not in smallest whole numbers.$a$),
  ('07fcb5de-d031-47bd-84f9-346fc47457cd', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Treats acetic acid as a strong acid and writes it as free H+, collapsing the equation to the strong-acid/strong-base form and erasing the intact weak-acid molecule.$a$),
  ('07fcb5de-d031-47bd-84f9-346fc47457cd', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Fails to dissociate the strong base Ba(OH)2, keeping it as an intact molecular unit instead of splitting it into Ba2+ and OH- as a strong electrolyte.$a$);

-- 9. [Net Ionic Equations & Spectator Ions · Skill 2 · ans B] id 80e8611c-1b8a-4c50-a660-b65070f6ed15
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Equal volumes of \(0.20\,\text{M}\) aqueous ammonium phosphate, \((\text{NH}_4)_3\text{PO}_4\), and \(0.20\,\text{M}\) aqueous calcium nitrate, \(\text{Ca(NO}_3)_2\), are combined, and a single precipitate forms. Treating every soluble salt as a fully dissociated strong electrolyte, which choice most likely represents the correctly balanced net ionic equation for the reaction that occurs?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + \\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{CaPO}_4(s)\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(3\\,\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 2\\,\\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca}_3(\\text{PO}_4)_2(s)\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(3\\,\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 6\\,\\text{NO}_3^-(aq) \\rightarrow 3\\,\\text{Ca(NO}_3)_2(s)\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(3\\,\\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 6\\,\\text{NO}_3^-(aq) + 6\\,\\text{NH}_4^+(aq) + 2\\,\\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca}_3(\\text{PO}_4)_2(s) + 6\\,\\text{NH}_4^+(aq) + 6\\,\\text{NO}_3^-(aq)\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the net ionic equation can be reached only after three chained steps: (1) decide which of the two possible exchange products is insoluble, (2) dissociate every soluble strong electrolyte and identify the spectator ions, and (3) charge-balance the resulting precipitate. The two conceivable exchange products are \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\) and \(\text{NH}_4\text{NO}_3\). By the solubility rules, all nitrates and all ammonium salts are soluble, so \(\text{NH}_4\text{NO}_3\) stays dissolved; phosphates are insoluble except those of group 1 metals and ammonium, so the calcium phosphate is the precipitate. Dissociating the reactants gives \(2\,(\text{NH}_4)_3\text{PO}_4 \rightarrow 6\,\text{NH}_4^+ + 2\,\text{PO}_4^{3-}\) and \(3\,\text{Ca(NO}_3)_2 \rightarrow 3\,\text{Ca}^{2+} + 6\,\text{NO}_3^-\). The complete ionic equation is \(3\,\text{Ca}^{2+} + 6\,\text{NO}_3^- + 6\,\text{NH}_4^+ + 2\,\text{PO}_4^{3-} \rightarrow \text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2(s) + 6\,\text{NH}_4^+ + 6\,\text{NO}_3^-\). The \(6\,\text{NH}_4^+\) and \(6\,\text{NO}_3^-\) appear unchanged on both sides, so they are the spectator ions and cancel, leaving \(3\,\text{Ca}^{2+}(aq) + 2\,\text{PO}_4^{3-}(aq) \rightarrow \text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2(s)\). The \(3:2\) cation-to-anion ratio is fixed by charge neutrality: \(3(+2) + 2(-3) = +6 - 6 = 0\), matching the neutral solid and reproducing the formula \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\). (Choice A) This collapses the coefficients to a \(1:1\) ratio and invents \(\text{CaPO}_4\), a species whose \(+2\) and \(-3\) ion charges sum to \(-1\) rather than zero; the precipitate is not charge-balanced and contradicts the \(3:2\) stoichiometry demanded by the \(2+\) and \(3-\) charges, so the correct solid must be \(\text{Ca}_3(\text{PO}_4)_2\). (Choice C) This precipitates \(\text{Ca(NO}_3)_2\), reversing the relevant solubility rule; nitrate salts and calcium nitrate in particular are freely soluble, so \(\text{NO}_3^-\) is a spectator and never forms a solid here, while the genuinely insoluble phosphate is ignored. (Choice D) This is the complete ionic equation written before cancellation; it correctly shows every dissociated species and the intact precipitate but omits the final step of removing the \(6\,\text{NH}_4^+\) and \(6\,\text{NO}_3^-\) spectator ions that appear identically on both sides, so it is not yet the net ionic equation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply solubility rules to identify which of two candidate products precipitates, locate the spectator ions, and then charge-balance the solid before writing the final net ionic equation.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '80e8611c-1b8a-4c50-a660-b65070f6ed15';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '80e8611c-1b8a-4c50-a660-b65070f6ed15';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('80e8611c-1b8a-4c50-a660-b65070f6ed15', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Drops the charge-balance constraint and writes a 1:1 formula CaPO4, ignoring that the 2+ and 3- ion charges fix a 3:2 ratio$a$),
  ('80e8611c-1b8a-4c50-a660-b65070f6ed15', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the solubility rule and precipitates the soluble nitrate (Ca(NO3)2) instead of recognizing nitrates are always soluble spectators, missing the insoluble phosphate$a$),
  ('80e8611c-1b8a-4c50-a660-b65070f6ed15', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Writes the complete ionic equation correctly but fails the final step of canceling the NH4+ and NO3- spectator ions to produce the net ionic equation$a$);

-- 10. [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules · Skill 2 · ans B] id 65d51416-1ff8-4ada-acad-413cb066054e
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$During charging, the cathode material \(\text{LiCoO}_2\) is partially delithiated to \(\text{Li}_{0.5}\text{CoO}_2\), in which cobalt is present as a mixture of \(\text{Co}^{3+}\) and \(\text{Co}^{4+}\). Treating lithium and oxygen as having their usual oxidation numbers, what is the average oxidation state of cobalt in \(\text{Li}_{0.5}\text{CoO}_2\)?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+3\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+3.5\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+4\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+4.5\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the average oxidation state must be obtained by chaining two assignment rules with the charge-neutrality constraint, where the controlling detail is the fractional lithium subscript. First, lithium takes its usual oxidation number of \(+1\), and with a subscript of \(0.5\) it contributes \(0.5 \times (+1) = +0.5\). Next, oxygen takes its usual oxidation number of \(-2\), and the two oxygen atoms contribute \(2 \times (-2) = -4\). Because \(\text{Li}_{0.5}\text{CoO}_2\) is a neutral compound, the sum of all oxidation numbers must equal zero, so the single cobalt center must satisfy \(0.5 + x + (-4) = 0 \Rightarrow x = +3.5\). This non-integer average is physically meaningful: setting the fraction of cobalt present as \(\text{Co}^{4+}\) equal to \(f\) gives \(3(1-f) + 4f = 3 + f = 3.5\), so \(f = 0.5\), meaning half of the cobalt is \(\text{Co}^{3+}\) and half is \(\text{Co}^{4+}\), consistent with removing half of the \(+1\) lithium and oxidizing an equal amount of cobalt. (Choice A) \(+3\) results from ignoring the fractional subscript and treating the solid as fully lithiated \(\text{LiCoO}_2\), where \((+1) + x + (-4) = 0\) gives \(x = +3\); this drops the controlling delithiation constraint and yields the value before any cobalt is oxidized. (Choice C) \(+4\) results from omitting the lithium contribution entirely and balancing cobalt against only the oxygen, \(x + (-4) = 0 \Rightarrow x = +4\); failing to invoke the alkali-metal \(+1\) rule overstates the average because the residual \(+0.5\) from lithium is left out. (Choice D) \(+4.5\) results from a sign error in which the lithium contribution is subtracted rather than added, \(x - 0.5 - 4 = 0 \Rightarrow x = +4.5\); assigning lithium an effective \(-0.5\) reverses the polarity of an electropositive metal that can only donate electrons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the oxidation-number rules for two different elements with the overall charge-balance condition and correctly handle a fractional stoichiometric coefficient to arrive at the average oxidation state.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '65d51416-1ff8-4ada-acad-413cb066054e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '65d51416-1ff8-4ada-acad-413cb066054e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('65d51416-1ff8-4ada-acad-413cb066054e', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Dropped the controlling fractional subscript and treated the material as fully lithiated LiCoO2, giving the pre-oxidation value +3$a$),
  ('65d51416-1ff8-4ada-acad-413cb066054e', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Omitted the alkali-metal +1 contribution entirely and balanced cobalt against oxygen alone, giving +4$a$),
  ('65d51416-1ff8-4ada-acad-413cb066054e', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Sign slip that subtracts the lithium contribution instead of adding it, treating electropositive Li as if it carried negative charge, giving +4.5$a$);

-- 11. [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules · Skill 2 · ans C] id 89a84160-8f09-4147-a1e2-d206b37d3ef9
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Potassium peroxymonosulfate (the oxidant in Oxone) contains the peroxymonosulfate ion, \(\text{HSO}_5^-\), whose connectivity is \(\text{H}-\text{O}-\text{O}-\text{SO}_3\): a single peroxide \(\text{O}-\text{O}\) linkage bridges the acidic hydrogen to a sulfur center bearing three additional oxygen atoms. Assigning oxidation numbers by the standard rules, what is the oxidation number of sulfur in \(\text{HSO}_5^-\)?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+7\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+8\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+6\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+3\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the five oxygen atoms in \(\text{HSO}_5^-\) are not all equivalent: the connectivity \(\text{H}-\text{O}-\text{O}-\text{SO}_3\) shows one peroxide \(\text{O}-\text{O}\) linkage, so two of the oxygens are peroxide oxygens (assigned \(-1\) each, the named exception) while the remaining three, bonded only to sulfur, take the usual \(-2\). The hydrogen is bonded to the more electronegative oxygen, so it is \(+1\). The sum of all oxidation numbers must equal the ion's charge of \(-1\). Letting \(x\) be the oxidation number of sulfur: \((+1) + x + 2(-1) + 3(-2) = -1\), which gives \(1 + x - 2 - 6 = -1\), so \(x - 7 = -1\) and \(x = +6\). The intermediate result that two oxygens are \(-1\) rather than \(-2\) is what feeds the final sulfur value; sulfur sits at its maximum \(+6\), consistent with a sulfate-derived center. (Choice A) \(+7\) results from recognizing the linkage but counting only one of the two bridging oxygens as a peroxide oxygen, leaving four oxygens at \(-2\): \((+1) + x + 1(-1) + 4(-2) = -1\) gives \(x = +7\); both atoms of an \(\text{O}-\text{O}\) bond are peroxide oxygens and must each be \(-1\). (Choice B) \(+8\) results from dropping the peroxide constraint and treating all five oxygens as \(-2\): \((+1) + x + 5(-2) = -1\) gives \(x = +8\), a value impossible for sulfur, whose highest accessible oxidation number is \(+6\) (it has only six valence electrons); the impossibility is the signal that the \(\text{O}-\text{O}\) linkage was missed. (Choice D) \(+3\) results from over-applying the exception and assigning every oxygen \(-1\): \((+1) + x + 5(-1) = -1\) gives \(x = +3\); only oxygens within an \(\text{O}-\text{O}\) bond are peroxide oxygens, and the three oxygens bonded solely to sulfur retain the standard \(-2\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify which oxygens are governed by the peroxide exception, assign the rest by the default rule, and chain those values through the charge-balance equation to back-solve for sulfur.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '89a84160-8f09-4147-a1e2-d206b37d3ef9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '89a84160-8f09-4147-a1e2-d206b37d3ef9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('89a84160-8f09-4147-a1e2-d206b37d3ef9', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Partial application of the exception: counts only one atom of the O-O bond as a peroxide oxygen (-1) while leaving its partner at -2, yielding S = +7$a$),
  ('89a84160-8f09-4147-a1e2-d206b37d3ef9', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Dropped peroxide constraint: applies the default oxygen rule (all O = -2) and never recognizes the O-O linkage, yielding the impossible S = +8$a$),
  ('89a84160-8f09-4147-a1e2-d206b37d3ef9', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Over-generalized exception: assigns -1 to all five oxygens rather than only the two in the peroxide bridge, yielding S = +3$a$);

-- 12. [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules · Skill 2 · ans D] id a3278e72-473b-4a29-9fa1-fb7a348171f6
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Calcium hydride reacts with water according to \(\text{CaH}_2(s) + 2\,\text{H}_2\text{O}(l) \rightarrow \text{Ca(OH)}_2(aq) + 2\,\text{H}_2(g)\). A \(5.05\ \text{g}\) sample of \(\text{CaH}_2\) (molar mass \(42.10\ \text{g/mol}\)) is consumed completely. Treating the reaction by its oxidation-number changes, how many moles of electrons are transferred?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.120\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.480\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.252\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.240\\ \\text{mol}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the problem must be solved by first assigning oxidation numbers, then identifying which atoms actually change state, and finally scaling by the moles of reactant. In \(\text{CaH}_2\) hydrogen is bonded to a less electronegative metal, so each hydridic hydrogen is assigned \(-1\) (and calcium is \(+2\), giving the required neutral sum \((+2) + 2(-1) = 0\)). In water and in \(\text{Ca(OH)}_2\) hydrogen is \(+1\); in \(\text{H}_2\) it is \(0\). Comparing reactants to products, calcium stays \(+2\) and oxygen stays \(-2\), so the only redox change is in hydrogen: the two hydridic H go \(-1 \rightarrow 0\) (oxidation, \(2\) electrons lost) while two of the water hydrogens go \(+1 \rightarrow 0\) (reduction, \(2\) electrons gained). The electrons lost equal the electrons gained, so exactly \(2\) electrons are transferred per formula unit of \(\text{CaH}_2\). Converting mass to moles, \(5.05\ \text{g} \div 42.10\ \text{g/mol} = 0.120\ \text{mol}\ \text{CaH}_2\), and multiplying by \(2\) electrons per formula unit gives \(2 \times 0.120 = 0.240\ \text{mol}\) of electrons transferred. (Choice A) This value, \(0.120\ \text{mol}\), is the moles of \(\text{CaH}_2\) itself; it follows from counting only \(1\) electron per formula unit, which underestimates the transfer because both hydridic hydrogens (not one) are oxidized from \(-1\) to \(0\). (Choice B) This value, \(0.480\ \text{mol}\), double-counts the electrons by summing the \(2\) electrons lost by the hydrides and the \(2\) electrons gained by the water hydrogens to get \(4\) per formula unit; in a balanced redox process electrons lost equal electrons gained, so the number transferred is \(2\), not their sum. (Choice C) This value, \(0.252\ \text{mol}\), results from computing the moles of \(\text{CaH}_2\) with a molar mass of only \(40.08\ \text{g/mol}\) (the mass of calcium alone, omitting the two hydrogen atoms), yielding \(0.126\ \text{mol}\) and then \(2 \times 0.126\); the hydrogen mass cannot be dropped because those very hydrogens are the redox-active atoms. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain an oxidation-number assignment to a redox electron count and then to a mass-to-mole stoichiometric conversion.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'a3278e72-473b-4a29-9fa1-fb7a348171f6';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a3278e72-473b-4a29-9fa1-fb7a348171f6';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a3278e72-473b-4a29-9fa1-fb7a348171f6', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Counts only one electron per CaH2 (treats a single hydride as oxidized), returning the moles of CaH2 itself instead of moles of electrons$a$),
  ('a3278e72-473b-4a29-9fa1-fb7a348171f6', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Double-counts by adding electrons lost (2) and electrons gained (2) to get 4 per formula unit, instead of recognizing electrons transferred equals electrons lost equals electrons gained$a$),
  ('a3278e72-473b-4a29-9fa1-fb7a348171f6', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Uses the molar mass of calcium alone (40.08 g/mol), dropping the mass of the two redox-active hydrogens before the mole conversion$a$);

-- 13. [Oxidation Numbers: Assignment & Rules · Skill 2 · ans A] id a793d310-bf4f-4c60-8171-d70d3fc28501
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Oxygen difluoride reacts with water vapor according to the balanced equation \(\text{OF}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{O}_2 + 2\,\text{HF}\). Fluorine is the most electronegative element and is assigned \(-1\) wherever it appears here. Treating each oxygen atom individually, which statement most accurately describes the oxidation-number changes the two oxygen atoms undergo as they combine into \(\text{O}_2\)?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"The oxygen from \\(\\text{OF}_2\\) drops from \\(+2\\) to \\(0\\) while the oxygen from \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) rises from \\(-2\\) to \\(0\\), so oxygen is simultaneously reduced and oxidized."},{"label":"B","text":"Both oxygen atoms begin at \\(-2\\) and rise to \\(0\\), so oxygen is oxidized while fluorine is reduced from \\(0\\) to \\(-1\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"The oxygen from \\(\\text{OF}_2\\) rises from \\(-2\\) to \\(0\\) while the oxygen from \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) drops from \\(+2\\) to \\(0\\), so each oxygen moves toward the other's starting state."},{"label":"D","text":"Both oxygen atoms begin at \\(+2\\) and drop to \\(0\\), so oxygen is reduced while hydrogen is oxidized from \\(+1\\) to \\(0\\)."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the two oxygen atoms enter the reaction in two different oxidation states and must be assigned separately. In \(\text{OF}_2\), oxygen is bonded to fluorine, the most electronegative element; the shared electrons are formally assigned to fluorine, so each fluorine is \(-1\) and, by the neutrality condition \(x + 2(-1) = 0\), the oxygen is \(x = +2\) (the standard exception to the usual \(-2\) rule). In \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), oxygen is bonded to the less electronegative hydrogen, so it takes its usual \(-2\) while each hydrogen is \(+1\). In the product \(\text{O}_2\), an element in its elemental form, every oxygen is \(0\). Tracking each atom: the oxygen originating in \(\text{OF}_2\) goes from \(+2\) to \(0\) (it gains electrons, i.e., is reduced), while the oxygen originating in \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) goes from \(-2\) to \(0\) (it loses electrons, i.e., is oxidized). The same element is thus both oxidized and reduced, with the two starting states converging on the single intermediate value \(0\); fluorine \((-1 \rightarrow -1)\) and hydrogen \((+1 \rightarrow +1)\) are unchanged spectators to the electron transfer. (Choice B) This choice drops the fluorine exception and assigns the \(\text{OF}_2\) oxygen its usual \(-2\); it then claims fluorine is reduced, but fluorine is already \(-1\) in \(\text{OF}_2\) and remains \(-1\) in \(\text{HF}\), so it does not change at all, and treating both oxygens as starting at \(-2\) wrongly makes the process a simple oxidation with no reduction partner. (Choice C) This choice correctly identifies that one oxygen is \(+2\) and one is \(-2\) but assigns the values to the wrong sources, placing \(+2\) on the water oxygen and \(-2\) on the \(\text{OF}_2\) oxygen; oxygen carries the positive state only when bonded to the more electronegative fluorine, so the \(+2\) belongs to the \(\text{OF}_2\) oxygen, making the described directions of change reversed. (Choice D) This choice applies the fluorine exception to both oxygens, assigning \(+2\) even to the water oxygen, which is bonded only to less electronegative hydrogen and must be \(-2\); it then invents an oxidation of hydrogen, but hydrogen is \(+1\) in both \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) and \(\text{HF}\) and does not change. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the electronegativity-based oxidation-number rules to two distinct oxygen environments, recognize the fluorine exception in only one of them, and then chain those assignments to the elemental product to track each atom's individual change.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'a793d310-bf4f-4c60-8171-d70d3fc28501';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a793d310-bf4f-4c60-8171-d70d3fc28501';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a793d310-bf4f-4c60-8171-d70d3fc28501', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Drops the fluorine exception, assigning both oxygens the usual -2 and then forcing fluorine (already -1, unchanged) to be the reduced species$a$),
  ('a793d310-bf4f-4c60-8171-d70d3fc28501', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Recognizes the +2/-2 split but swaps which oxygen source carries which value, reversing the direction of each atom's oxidation-state change$a$),
  ('a793d310-bf4f-4c60-8171-d70d3fc28501', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Over-applies the fluorine exception to every oxygen (both at +2) and fabricates an oxidation of hydrogen, which is +1 in both reactant and product$a$);

-- 14. [Oxidation/Reduction & Identifying Agents · Skill 2 · ans D] id 0bd84031-e775-4253-8341-b89b52aeb326
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$When zinc metal is added to dilute nitric acid, no hydrogen gas is evolved; instead the metal dissolves as the nitrate nitrogen is reduced to nitric oxide, according to the balanced equation \(3\,\text{Zn} + 8\,\text{HNO}_3 \rightarrow 3\,\text{Zn(NO}_3)_2 + 2\,\text{NO} + 4\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\). A student is asked to identify the oxidizing agent and to report the total number of moles of electrons transferred per mole of this balanced equation. Which choice is most likely correct?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{H}^+\\) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(2\\) mol of electrons are transferred, because the acid's protons are reduced to elemental hydrogen."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{HNO}_3\\) (via nitrate nitrogen) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(8\\) mol of electrons are transferred, because each reduced nitrogen falls from \\(+5\\) to \\(-3\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Zn}\\) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(6\\) mol of electrons are transferred, because the three zinc atoms each lose two electrons."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{HNO}_3\\) (via nitrate nitrogen) is the oxidizing agent, and \\(6\\) mol of electrons are transferred, because each reduced nitrogen falls from \\(+5\\) to \\(+2\\)."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because identifying the oxidizing agent here requires recognizing that in nitric-acid chemistry the oxidant is the nitrogen of the nitrate, not the acidic proton, and then counting electrons from the oxidation-state changes through the stoichiometry of the balanced equation. First, locate the species whose oxidation state falls. Zinc rises from \(0\) to \(+2\), so Zn is oxidized and is the reducing agent. The protons are not reduced—no \(\text{H}_2\) is evolved (the stem states this explicitly), so \(\text{H}^+\) acts only as the acid, not as the oxidant. Of the eight nitrogen atoms entering as \(\text{HNO}_3\) (all at \(+5\)), six emerge unchanged at \(+5\) in the three \(\text{Zn(NO}_3)_2\) units (which contain \(3 \times 2 = 6\) nitrate ions); only the two nitrogen atoms that become \(\text{NO}\) are reduced. In \(\text{NO}\), oxygen is \(-2\) and the molecule is neutral, so that nitrogen is \(+2\). Each reduced nitrogen therefore changes \(+5 \rightarrow +2\), a gain of \(3\) electrons, and with two such nitrogen atoms the reduction accepts \(2 \times 3 = 6\) electrons. Cross-check with the oxidation side: three Zn each lose \(2\) electrons for \(3 \times 2 = 6\) electrons released, exactly matching the \(6\) electrons accepted. The oxidizing agent is \(\text{HNO}_3\) (acting through its nitrate nitrogen), and \(6\) mol of electrons are transferred per mole of equation. (Choice A) names \(\text{H}^+\) as the oxidizing agent and assumes proton reduction to \(\text{H}_2\); however, with dilute nitric acid no hydrogen gas is produced, so the protons are not reduced, and \(2\) electrons would be the count only for a hypothetical \(\text{H}_2\)-evolving reaction that does not occur here. (Choice B) correctly identifies \(\text{HNO}_3\) as the oxidant but uses the wrong product depth: a \(+5 \rightarrow -3\) change corresponds to reduction all the way to the ammonium ion \(\text{NH}_4^+\), which forms only with very dilute acid, not to the \(\text{NO}\) (\(+2\)) specified by this equation, so it gives \(8\) electrons per reduced nitrogen instead of the \(3\) that apply here. (Choice C) reverses the agent roles: zinc's oxidation state increases, so zinc is oxidized and is the reducing agent, not the oxidizing agent, even though the \(6\)-electron total it cites happens to equal the electrons zinc releases. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a balance check of the equation, the identification of the true oxidant in nitric-acid chemistry, the oxidation-state assignment of nitrogen in nitric oxide, and the electron stoichiometry to arrive at a single self-consistent answer.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '0bd84031-e775-4253-8341-b89b52aeb326';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '0bd84031-e775-4253-8341-b89b52aeb326';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('0bd84031-e775-4253-8341-b89b52aeb326', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Treats H+ as the oxidizing agent and assumes proton reduction to H2 (2 e-), even though dilute HNO3 evolves no hydrogen gas$a$),
  ('0bd84031-e775-4253-8341-b89b52aeb326', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Names HNO3 correctly but uses the wrong reduction product (NH4+, +5->-3, 8 e-) instead of NO (+5->+2, 3 e-) given in the equation$a$),
  ('0bd84031-e775-4253-8341-b89b52aeb326', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Labels Zn (the oxidized reducing agent) as the oxidizing agent; the 6 e- value coincidentally matches the electrons Zn releases$a$);

-- 15. [Oxidation/Reduction & Identifying Agents · Skill 2 · ans B] id 72c91417-e76b-4080-8bf4-98419bb41184
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$In acidic solution, dichromate cleanly oxidizes 2-propanol (isopropyl alcohol) to acetone, \(\text{CH}_3\text{CH(OH)CH}_3 \rightarrow \text{CH}_3\text{C(=O)CH}_3\), as chromium is reduced from \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\) to \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\). A chemist drives the reaction until \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of 2-propanol has been fully converted to acetone. If the dichromate is the limiting electron acceptor and is consumed only by this oxidation, how many moles of \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\) are reduced?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.20\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.10\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.30\\ \\text{mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(0.050\\ \\text{mol}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the problem must be chained through three steps. First, assign the oxidation state of the functionalized (carbinol) carbon. In 2-propanol that central carbon is bonded to two carbons (each contributes \(0\)), one hydrogen (\(-1\)), and one oxygen of the hydroxyl (\(+1\)), giving \(2(0) + (-1) + (+1) = 0\). In acetone that same carbon is the carbonyl carbon, bonded to two carbons (\(0\)) and double-bonded to oxygen (two bonds to O contribute \(+2\)), giving \(2(0) + (+2) = +2\). The carbon therefore rises from \(0\) to \(+2\), a loss of \(2\) electrons per molecule of 2-propanol oxidized. Second, count the electrons accepted per dichromate: each chromium falls from \(+6\) in \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\) to \(+3\) in \(\text{Cr}^{3+}\), gaining \(3\) electrons, and because there are two chromium atoms per dichromate, each \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\) accepts \(6\) electrons. Third, balance electrons stoichiometrically: \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of 2-propanol releases \(0.30 \times 2 = 0.60\ \text{mol}\) of electrons, and dividing by the \(6\) electrons accepted per dichromate gives \(0.60 / 6 = 0.10\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}\). (Choice A) This value results from dividing the \(0.60\ \text{mol}\) of electrons by \(3\) rather than \(6\), counting the electrons accepted by only a single chromium atom and neglecting that the dichromate ion contains two reducible chromium centers, each gaining \(3\) electrons for a total of \(6\). (Choice C) This value comes from assuming a one-to-one mole correspondence between the alcohol oxidized and the dichromate reduced, ignoring the electron balance entirely; matching electrons lost by carbon (2 per alcohol) to electrons gained by dichromate (6 per ion) is required because the two species exchange electrons in a fixed ratio, not in a 1:1 molar ratio. (Choice D) This value arises from treating the carbinol carbon as losing only \(1\) electron, as if converting the alcohol to the ketone removed a single hydrogen; the transformation removes the equivalent of two hydrogens and the carbon's oxidation state rises by two units (\(0 \rightarrow +2\)), so each alcohol releases \(2\) electrons, not \(1\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain an atom-by-atom oxidation-state assignment into an electron-count for the oxidant and then into a stoichiometric mole ratio, where each intermediate result feeds the next.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '72c91417-e76b-4080-8bf4-98419bb41184';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '72c91417-e76b-4080-8bf4-98419bb41184';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('72c91417-e76b-4080-8bf4-98419bb41184', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Counts electrons accepted by only one chromium atom (3 e-) instead of per dichromate ion (6 e-), forgetting the ion contains two reducible Cr centers$a$),
  ('72c91417-e76b-4080-8bf4-98419bb41184', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Assumes a 1:1 mole ratio between alcohol oxidized and dichromate reduced, ignoring the electron balance that fixes the actual stoichiometric ratio$a$),
  ('72c91417-e76b-4080-8bf4-98419bb41184', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Assigns the carbinol carbon a one-electron change (as if one H is removed), undercounting the oxidation-state rise of 0 to +2 that releases two electrons per alcohol$a$);

-- 16. [Oxidation/Reduction & Identifying Agents · Skill 2 · ans A] id e136b581-4775-49fe-97f0-2df2b447d632
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A plant runs two parallel chlorine-disproportionation lines, each consuming the same \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{Cl}_2\). Line H bubbles \(\text{Cl}_2\) through hot concentrated NaOH, where chlorine disproportionates to chloride (\(\text{Cl}^-\)) and chlorate (\(\text{ClO}_3^-\)); Line C bubbles it through cold dilute NaOH, where chlorine disproportionates to chloride (\(\text{Cl}^-\)) and hypochlorite (\(\text{ClO}^-\)). Counting electrons transferred only once per redox event, how many more moles of electrons does Line H transfer than Line C for the \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{Cl}_2\)?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.20\\) mol of electrons"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(0.50\\) mol of electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(0.40\\) mol of electrons"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\tfrac{2}{3}\\) mol of electrons"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the two pathways differ in the oxidation state reached by the oxidized chlorine, and that difference must be carried through a per-mole electron count and then scaled to the common \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{Cl}_2\). In both reactions every chlorine atom enters at oxidation state \(0\) in \(\text{Cl}_2\). For Line H, of the \(6\) Cl atoms per \(3\ \text{mol}\ \text{Cl}_2\), the \(5\) atoms in \(\text{NaCl}\) are reduced \(0 \rightarrow -1\) (gaining \(5\) electrons total) and the \(1\) atom in \(\text{NaClO}_3\) is oxidized \(0 \rightarrow +5\) (Na is \(+1\) and three O at \(-2\) sum to \(-6\), leaving Cl at \(+5\)), losing \(5\) electrons; electrons lost equal electrons gained, so \(5\) mol of electrons move per \(3\ \text{mol}\ \text{Cl}_2\), i.e. \(\frac{5}{3}\) mol of electrons per mole of \(\text{Cl}_2\). For Line C, of the \(2\) Cl atoms per mole of \(\text{Cl}_2\), one is reduced \(0 \rightarrow -1\) and one is oxidized \(0 \rightarrow +1\) (Na \(+1\) and O \(-2\) leave Cl at \(+1\) in \(\text{NaClO}\)), so exactly \(1\) mol of electrons moves per mole of \(\text{Cl}_2\). For \(0.30\ \text{mol}\ \text{Cl}_2\): Line H transfers \(0.30 \times \frac{5}{3} = 0.50\) mol of electrons and Line C transfers \(0.30 \times 1 = 0.30\) mol of electrons, a difference of \(0.50 - 0.30 = 0.20\) mol of electrons. (Choice B) is the total electrons transferred by Line H alone (\(0.30 \times \frac{5}{3} = 0.50\)); it results from forgetting to subtract the Line C baseline that the question asks to compare against. (Choice C) arises from double-counting the redox event in each line: adding the electrons lost to the electrons gained gives \(\frac{10}{3}\) per mole \(\text{Cl}_2\) for Line H and \(2\) for Line C, and \(0.30 \times (\frac{10}{3} - 2) = 0.40\); in any redox process the electrons released are the same electrons captured and must be counted only once. (Choice D) is the per-mole difference \(\frac{5}{3} - 1 = \frac{2}{3}\) left unscaled; it results from omitting the multiplication by the \(0.30\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{Cl}_2\) actually consumed, reporting a per-mole quantity where a total is required. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify that the controlling variable is the product oxidation state set by reaction conditions, derive a distinct per-mole electron count for each pathway, and chain those values through a scaled subtraction to reach the comparison.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'e136b581-4775-49fe-97f0-2df2b447d632';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'e136b581-4775-49fe-97f0-2df2b447d632';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('e136b581-4775-49fe-97f0-2df2b447d632', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Computes the Line H total correctly but omits the final subtraction of the Line C baseline, reporting one pathway's absolute value in place of the requested difference.$a$),
  ('e136b581-4775-49fe-97f0-2df2b447d632', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Double-counts the transferred electrons by summing electrons lost and electrons gained for each pathway, treating released and captured electrons as separate populations.$a$),
  ('e136b581-4775-49fe-97f0-2df2b447d632', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Finds the correct per-mole electron difference (5/3 minus 1) but forgets to multiply by the 0.30 mol of Cl2 consumed, reporting a per-mole quantity instead of a total.$a$);

-- 17. [Reaction Types & Classification · Skill 2 · ans C] id 738d3912-3c12-4e29-8b12-d8898d407f66
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$An industrial plant makes sulfuric acid in three consecutive steps: (1) \(\text{S}(s) + \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow \text{SO}_2(g)\); (2) \(2\,\text{SO}_2(g) + \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2\,\text{SO}_3(g)\); (3) \(\text{SO}_3(g) + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4(aq)\). Each step releases heat, and step 3 fumes and sharply lowers the pH. A student under time pressure must decide which step is the only one that is NOT an oxidation-reduction reaction. Which step is non-redox, and which feature most directly justifies that classification?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"Step 1, because both reactants are already in their standard states, so no element can change oxidation number when they combine."},{"label":"B","text":"Step 2, because the same two elements (sulfur and oxygen) appear on both sides, leaving every oxidation number unchanged across the arrow."},{"label":"C","text":"Step 3, because sulfur stays at \\(+6\\), oxygen at \\(-2\\), and hydrogen at \\(+1\\) on both sides, so no oxidation number changes despite the dramatic acid-forming chemistry."},{"label":"D","text":"Step 3, because the steep drop in pH shows protons are exchanged rather than electrons, and proton exchange is what excludes a reaction from being redox."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the only reliable test for redox versus non-redox is to assign oxidation numbers to every element in all three steps and find the single step in which nothing changes. In step 1, elemental \(\text{S}\) and \(\text{O}_2\) each enter at \(0\); in \(\text{SO}_2\) oxygen is \(-2\) (so \(0 + S + 2(-2) = 0\) gives \(S = +4\)), so sulfur goes \(0 \rightarrow +4\) and oxygen goes \(0 \rightarrow -2\): step 1 is redox. In step 2, sulfur in \(\text{SO}_2\) is \(+4\) and in \(\text{SO}_3\) is \(+6\) (since \(S + 3(-2) = 0\)), while the added \(\text{O}_2\) goes from \(0\) to \(-2\); sulfur is oxidized \(+4 \rightarrow +6\) and oxygen is reduced \(0 \rightarrow -2\): step 2 is also redox. In step 3, sulfur is \(+6\) in \(\text{SO}_3\) and remains \(+6\) in \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\) (for the neutral acid \(2(+1) + S + 4(-2) = 0\) gives \(S = +6\)); oxygen stays \(-2\) and hydrogen stays \(+1\) throughout. With no oxidation number changing, no electrons are transferred, so step 3 is the lone non-redox step: it is the hydration of an acid anhydride (a Lewis acid-base addition), not an oxidation-reduction. (Choice A) Being in standard states does not exempt a reaction from redox; in step 1 the free elements \(\text{S}\) and \(\text{O}_2\) start at \(0\) and end at \(+4\) and \(-2\) in \(\text{SO}_2\), so step 1 is in fact a redox combination, not the non-redox step. (Choice B) The mere reappearance of the same elements does not freeze their oxidation numbers; in step 2 sulfur rises from \(+4\) to \(+6\) and the newly added \(\text{O}_2\) falls from \(0\) to \(-2\), so step 2 is redox. (Choice D) This identifies the correct step but for the wrong reason: it confuses proton transfer (a Brønsted acid-base concept) with electron transfer (redox); a falling pH signals \(\text{H}^+\) release and is irrelevant to oxidation numbers, and proton exchange neither defines nor excludes redox—the absence of any oxidation-number change is what makes step 3 non-redox. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must assign and compare oxidation numbers across three chained transformations and isolate the single step in which none of them change.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '738d3912-3c12-4e29-8b12-d8898d407f66';
