INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('738d3912-3c12-4e29-8b12-d8898d407f66', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Believing reactions between elements in their standard states cannot be redox, ignoring that combining free elements changes their oxidation numbers from 0$a$),
  ('738d3912-3c12-4e29-8b12-d8898d407f66', 'B', 'misconception', $a$Assuming that because the same elements appear on both sides their oxidation numbers must be unchanged, failing to recompute S (+4 to +6) and O (0 to -2) in step 2$a$),
  ('738d3912-3c12-4e29-8b12-d8898d407f66', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Correct step but justifying non-redox by proton transfer / pH change (a Bronsted acid-base criterion) instead of by the absence of any oxidation-number change$a$);

-- 18. [Redox Titrations · Skill 2 · ans D] id c5c725d6-d93f-42b5-b434-d38b2d53fd92
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A \(0.3150\ \text{g}\) sample of impure sodium oxalate, Na\(_2\)C\(_2\)O\(_4\) (molar mass \(134.00\ \text{g/mol}\); the only redox-active component), is dissolved in acid and treated with \(60.00\ \text{mL}\) of \(0.02000\ \text{M}\) KMnO\(_4\)—an excess—so that all of the oxalate is oxidized to CO\(_2\) while permanganate is reduced to Mn\(^{2+}\). The unreacted permanganate is then back-titrated with \(0.05000\ \text{M}\) Fe\(^{2+}\), reaching the disappearance of the purple color after \(30.00\ \text{mL}\); in this back-titration MnO\(_4^-\) and Fe\(^{2+}\) react in a \(1{:}5\) mole ratio, while permanganate and oxalate react in a \(2{:}5\) mole ratio. What is the mass percent of Na\(_2\)C\(_2\)O\(_4\) in the sample most nearly?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(15.31\\%\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(127.62\\%\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(38.29\\%\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(95.71\\%\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Titration (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the analysis chains a back-titration accounting step with two distinct redox stoichiometries before reaching mass percent. First, the total permanganate added is \(n_{\text{MnO}_4^-,\text{total}} = (0.02000\ \text{M})(0.06000\ \text{L}) = 1.200\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\). Because this is an excess, only part of it reacts with oxalate; the leftover is found from the back-titration. The Fe\(^{2+}\) consumed is \((0.05000\ \text{M})(0.03000\ \text{L}) = 1.500\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\), and with the \(1{:}5\) ratio (MnO\(_4^-\) gains \(5\,e^-\) per ion, Fe\(^{2+}\) loses \(1\,e^-\)), the unreacted permanganate is \(n_{\text{left}} = \tfrac{1}{5}(1.500\times10^{-3}) = 3.000\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}\). The permanganate that actually oxidized oxalate is the difference: \(n_{\text{react}} = 1.200\times10^{-3} - 3.000\times10^{-4} = 9.000\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}\). Applying the \(2{:}5\) ratio (balancing \(2\,\text{MnO}_4^- \times 5\,e^- = 5\,\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-} \times 2\,e^- = 10\,e^-\)) gives \(n_{\text{C}_2\text{O}_4^{2-}} = \tfrac{5}{2}(9.000\times10^{-4}) = 2.250\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\), all from the sodium oxalate. The mass is \((2.250\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol})(134.00\ \text{g/mol}) = 0.3015\ \text{g}\), so the mass percent is \(\dfrac{0.3015\ \text{g}}{0.3150\ \text{g}}\times100\% = 95.71\%\). (Choice A) \(15.31\%\) inverts the oxalate stoichiometry, multiplying the reacted permanganate by \(\tfrac{2}{5}\) instead of \(\tfrac{5}{2}\) to obtain \(3.600\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}\) oxalate and \(0.04824\ \text{g}\); because the \(2{:}5\) ratio makes oxalate the more abundant species, the permanganate moles must be scaled up, not down. (Choice B) \(127.62\%\) drops the back-titration step entirely, treating the full \(1.200\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\) of added permanganate as having reacted with oxalate to give \(\tfrac{5}{2}(1.200\times10^{-3}) = 3.000\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\) oxalate and \(0.4020\ \text{g}\); the result exceeding \(100\%\) is itself the signal that the excess permanganate quantified by the Fe\(^{2+}\) back-titration was never subtracted. (Choice C) \(38.29\%\) carries the correct reacted permanganate \(9.000\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}\) but assumes a \(1{:}1\) permanganate-to-oxalate ratio, yielding \(9.000\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol}\) oxalate and \(0.1206\ \text{g}\); this ignores the \(10\) transferred electrons that mandate \(5\) oxalates per \(2\) permanganates. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must recognize that the controlling constraint is excess titrant, use the back-titration to isolate the permanganate consumed by the analyte, and then chain two separate redox ratios through to mass percent.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'c5c725d6-d93f-42b5-b434-d38b2d53fd92';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c5c725d6-d93f-42b5-b434-d38b2d53fd92';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c5c725d6-d93f-42b5-b434-d38b2d53fd92', 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Inverts the oxalate stoichiometry, scaling permanganate moles by 2/5 instead of 5/2$a$),
  ('c5c725d6-d93f-42b5-b434-d38b2d53fd92', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Omits the back-titration subtraction, counting all added (excess) permanganate as reacting with the analyte, producing an impossible >100% mass percent$a$),
  ('c5c725d6-d93f-42b5-b434-d38b2d53fd92', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Performs the back-titration correctly but applies a 1:1 permanganate-to-oxalate ratio, ignoring the redox electron balance that fixes the 2:5 stoichiometry$a$);

